From 7f2a8e2df62e3b472f3f18bdb82441cf11d9b0c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 00:10:18 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] virtio-media: send USERPTR on the wire for REQBUFS too, not
 just QBUF

The wire is USERPTR-only, and REQBUFS was the one buffer ioctl still
leaking the user-space memory type through. It survived on the RPi4 by
luck: the proxy device model ignores rb->memory entirely (it rewrites
the host call to V4L2_MEMORY_MMAP regardless), so a DMABUF REQBUFS went
unnoticed there.

The RPi5 path does not ignore it. The device model's decoder mode,
which the RPi5 uses, has its own dec_reqbufs(), and it returns EINVAL
for any memory type but USERPTR -- same restriction as the proxy path,
for the same reason. So on the RPi5 a DMABUF queue would have died at
buffer allocation, before a single QBUF, with nothing in any log to
point at the memory type.

Substitute on the way out and restore before anything reads it --
including queue->memory, which has to record what user-space asked for
rather than what went on the wire, because DQBUF restores the memory
type from it.

The allow_userptr gate is deliberately left inspecting what USER-SPACE
asked for: a DMABUF queue does not need that module parameter, only a
user-space request for USERPTR does.

Found by reading the device model's decoder-mode code, not on hardware.

Change-Id: I6630dac2d2a11ece05a012df08db33de6719f6fc
Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 driver/virtio_media_ioctls.c | 27 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 27 insertions(+)

diff --git a/driver/virtio_media_ioctls.c b/driver/virtio_media_ioctls.c
index 7fc7386..a70ce25 100644
--- a/driver/virtio_media_ioctls.c
+++ b/driver/virtio_media_ioctls.c
@@ -842,6 +842,7 @@ static int virtio_media_reqbufs(struct file *file, void *fh,
 	struct v4l2_fh *vfh = file_to_v4l2_fh(file);
 	struct virtio_media_session *session = fh_to_session(vfh);
 	struct virtio_media_queue_state *queue;
+	u32 orig_memory;
 	int ret;
 
 	if (b->type > VIRTIO_MEDIA_LAST_QUEUE)
@@ -850,8 +851,34 @@ static int virtio_media_reqbufs(struct file *file, void *fh,
 	if (b->memory == V4L2_MEMORY_USERPTR && !virtio_media_allow_userptr)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
+	/*
+	 * REQBUFS goes out as USERPTR too, for the same reason QBUF does: the wire
+	 * is USERPTR-only. See scatterlist_builder_add_buffer_dmabuf().
+	 *
+	 * This is not cosmetic. The proxy device model happens to ignore rb->memory
+	 * (it rewrites the host call to MMAP regardless), so a DMABUF REQBUFS
+	 * survived there by luck. The decoder-mode device model does not: its
+	 * dec_reqbufs() returns EINVAL for any memory type but USERPTR, which is
+	 * where a DMABUF queue would have died on the RPi5 -- at buffer allocation,
+	 * before a single QBUF, with nothing to point at the cause.
+	 *
+	 * Note the allow_userptr gate above is deliberately left looking at what
+	 * user-space asked for. A DMABUF queue does not need that module parameter;
+	 * only a user-space request for USERPTR does.
+	 */
+	orig_memory = b->memory;
+	if (orig_memory == V4L2_MEMORY_DMABUF)
+		b->memory = V4L2_MEMORY_USERPTR;
+
 	ret = virtio_media_send_wr_ioctl(vfh, VIDIOC_REQBUFS, b, sizeof(*b),
 					 sizeof(*b));
+
+	/*
+	 * Restore before anything reads it -- including queue->memory below, which
+	 * must record what user-space asked for, not what went on the wire.
+	 */
+	b->memory = orig_memory;
+
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
